## Statement

Let $P\subset S^2$ be the finite projection graph of a regular link diagram. If $P$ is disconnected, there is a simple closed curve $\gamma\subset S^2\setminus P$ such that $P$ has points on both sides of $\gamma$.

## Statement references

[Calegari, *Introduction to Knot Theory*, §5.2 and Exercise 5.14](https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/notes/knot_theory.pdf)

[Menasco, *Closed incompressible surfaces in alternating knot and link complements* (1984), §2](https://www.scribd.com/document/471863717/1-s2-0-0040938384900235-main-2)

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A finite disconnected projection graph $P\subset S^2$.

[F1] A regular projection is a finite graph with finitely many transverse double points and no triple points; crossingless link components are embedded circles ([[def-regular-oriented-link-diagram]]).

## Proof
1.1 Choose one connected component $P_0$ of $P$. Around each of its finitely many vertices take a small closed disk, and around each edge take a narrow closed strip joining the incident vertex disks; for a crossingless circle take an annular neighborhood. Because the graph is finite and its distinct components are disjoint compact sets, the disks and strips can be chosen so that their union $N$ is a compact connected subsurface of $S^2$, contains $P_0$ in its interior, and misses every other component of $P$. [given, F1, construct]

2.1 Since $P$ is disconnected, some other component $P_1$ lies in a component $U$ of $S^2\setminus N$. This $U$ is a disk: a connected compact subsurface of the sphere has boundary a finite union of disjoint circles, and each component of its complement is a disk. Its boundary $\gamma=\partial U$ is a simple closed curve, lies in $S^2\setminus P$ because $N$ misses $P_1$ and all other projection components, and separates $P_1\subset U$ from $P_0\subset N$. [given, F1, step 1.1, construct]

3.1 The construction uses finitely many neighborhoods and applies also when $P_0$ is a crossingless circle; therefore it proves the asserted separation for every disconnected regular projection. [given, step 1.1, step 2.1, cases] ∎
